WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:09.122
A bridge-photo inspection agent on OpenCV 5 reads a quarter of the pixels and flags 1 critical finding.

2
00:00:09.172 --> 00:00:11.647
It will not sign it off. A person must.

3
00:00:12.297 --> 00:00:19.204
Drishyam, an Agentic Vision entry to the OpenCV AI Competition, powered by AWS.

4
00:00:20.254 --> 00:00:25.829
Built by Vaibhav Jangid, lead engineer, and Bhavya Dhoot, design and presentation.

5
00:00:26.429 --> 00:00:32.338
America has about 625,000 bridges, inspected at least every two years.

6
00:00:32.388 --> 00:00:37.085
One drone inspection took more than 2,500 photos of one bridge.

7
00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:41.492
Drishyam is for the engineer reviewing them: it chooses where to look,

8
00:00:41.502 --> 00:00:45.609
zooms in where its segmenter is unsure, drafts defects for review,

9
00:00:45.619 --> 00:00:48.676
and hands every critical finding to a person.

10
00:00:49.326 --> 00:00:54.872
Recorded 23 October on the live site: the trained v1 and its new look policy.

11
00:00:54.882 --> 00:00:58.979
Live on AWS: OpenCV 5 on an arm Lambda.

12
00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:03.086
Watch it inspect a bridge; a privacy pass finds nothing to blur.

13
00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:08.203
One survey look at the whole photo, at one eighth size, finds spalling and corrosion.

14
00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:11.616
One level finer, the spalling is big enough to be critical,

15
00:01:11.626 --> 00:01:14.642
but the rebar scores under the confidence floor.

16
00:01:14.652 --> 00:01:19.139
It takes one confirm look, centred on the rebar, and does not confirm it.

17
00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:23.744
So the rebar goes to a person to check by eye, not as a critical finding.

18
00:01:23.754 --> 00:01:27.876
Three more looks find a crack; with every region looked at, it stops.

19
00:01:28.376 --> 00:01:33.604
Only a reviewer's decision moves the order; in this recording, a script clicks Approve.

20
00:01:33.614 --> 00:01:39.258
The script also enters the rebar's check by eye, a defect; only then is the order ready.

21
00:01:39.268 --> 00:01:43.528
Change one character of the order's signature: the server refuses it.

22
00:01:44.078 --> 00:01:45.240
Now, break it.

23
00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.984
Slow the segmenter, and the governor switches to the fast model.

24
00:01:49.084 --> 00:01:52.725
The planner drill sends a shell command; the allow-list rejects it.

25
00:01:52.825 --> 00:01:57.269
KILL halts it before the next look; Resume replays the moves and carries on.

26
00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:01.491
No language model runs live; these planners are scripted to fail.

27
00:02:02.141 --> 00:02:07.275
Over MCP, Claude drove the same tools on the same photo, with v1.

28
00:02:07.325 --> 00:02:12.064
At call 4 the spall's two masks disagreed, so Claude handed it to a human.

29
00:02:12.114 --> 00:02:17.236
Then it looked at the crack, and stopped at call 19, at 17 % of the pixels.

30
00:02:17.286 --> 00:02:21.723
It found all four labelled defects, but it had read the labels beforehand.

31
00:02:22.373 --> 00:02:26.973
Each step is perception, decision and action, with a human gate at the end.

32
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:31.027
CloudFront and a rate-limiting firewall front the arm Lambda;

33
00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:34.667
DynamoDB keeps kill flags, Secrets Manager the key.

34
00:02:34.677 --> 00:02:40.797
OpenCV does the looking: a pyramid, saliency, warpPerspective as a pan-tilt-zoom camera,

35
00:02:40.807 --> 00:02:42.928
a blur gate, face blurring.

36
00:02:42.938 --> 00:02:47.968
The site now runs v1 on OpenCV 5's new DNN engine.

37
00:02:47.978 --> 00:02:54.494
On Lambda a step took under 90 ms at p95 then, and under 1.2 s now.

38
00:02:55.144 --> 00:02:57.508
With the classical segmenter,

39
00:02:57.518 --> 00:03:04.384
on 200 synthetic photos it finds 87 % of full tiling's recall at 26 % of the pixels,

40
00:03:04.394 --> 00:03:06.681
short of its 90 % target.

41
00:03:06.691 --> 00:03:09.118
One downscaled pass does about as well.

42
00:03:09.128 --> 00:03:13.107
Ablations: dropping the survey look loses recall everywhere;

43
00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:17.546
a random gaze order loses it on the larger and the real photos.

44
00:03:17.556 --> 00:03:22.759
On 200 real CODEBRIM photos, held out, the trained v1 finds 49 %:

45
00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:27.099
two and a half times the classical engine's 1 in 5.

46
00:03:27.109 --> 00:03:29.419
But its boxes find 30 % at random,

47
00:03:29.429 --> 00:03:33.103
and it draws five and a half times the critical boxes.

48
00:03:33.113 --> 00:03:38.766
A person signs about two critical findings a photo, about one with the live policy,

49
00:03:38.776 --> 00:03:40.653
and recall is unchanged.

50
00:03:40.663 --> 00:03:47.138
On 51 undamaged synthetic photos, the classical engine flags 2 in 3; v1, none.

51
00:03:47.788 --> 00:03:53.214
Our own wall, no ground truth: no region was proposed, so it looked at the whole photo.

52
00:03:53.224 --> 00:03:57.838
The segmenter saw crack pixels, but the blur gate refused this sharp view,

53
00:03:57.848 --> 00:03:59.598
and nothing was flagged.

54
00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:04.765
On a second wall, one close look flagged a crack on the lower of two we traced,

55
00:04:04.775 --> 00:04:07.489
and never looked closely at most of the wall.

56
00:04:08.339 --> 00:04:11.148
The classical engine's failures, in the open:

57
00:04:11.158 --> 00:04:15.855
graffiti read as spalling, a formwork line as a crack, glare, and blur.

58
00:04:15.905 --> 00:04:19.970
Only the glare gate caught its case; three fixes are not built yet.

59
00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:26.488
Pixels, not millimetres. AI-assisted, not an inspection. Only a person can approve.

60
00:04:27.538 --> 00:04:31.542
make test, make eval, and one script for the AWS stack.

61
00:04:32.092 --> 00:04:35.756
Drishyam. It looks where it matters, and asks a human first.
